## Interfaces
An interface defines a **contract** - what properties and methods an object must have, without implementing them:

```ts
interface Printable {
    toString(): string;
}

class User implements Printable {
    name: string;
    constructor(name: string) {
        this.name = name;
    }
    toString(): string {
        return "User: " + this.name;
    }
}
```

- `interface` defines the shape
- `implements` promises the class follows that shape
- If you forget a method, TypeScript gives a compile error

### Multiple interfaces:
```ts
interface Printable {
    toString(): string;
}

interface Saveable {
    save(): void;
}

class Document implements Printable, Saveable {
    toString(): string { return "doc"; }
    save(): void { /* save to disk */ }
}
```

Classes can only `extend` ONE class, but `implement` MANY interfaces.

### Interface vs Class:
- **Class**: has code (implementation)
- **Interface**: has no code (just the contract)

---
## Assignment:
Create an interface `Describable` with a method `describe(): string`.

Create a class `Planet` that implements `Describable` with:
- `name` (string) and `radius` (number) set via constructor
- `describe()` returns `"<name> has radius <radius> km"`

Create a class `Star` that implements `Describable` with:
- `name` (string) and `temp` (number) set via constructor
- `describe()` returns `"<name> burns at <temp>K"`

